2. Theoretical Load Model

2.1 Fundamental Distinction

In the passive Inductrack/PMEDS model, the magnetic drag F_D decelerates the vehicle. The power P_D = F_D v is drawn from the kinetic energy of the vehicle, not from the grid. It is dissipated as heat in the track through induced eddy currents.

\underbrace{P_D}_{\text{mechanical loss of the vehicle}} \neq \underbrace{P_{\rm elec,road}}_{\text{electrical power drawn from the grid}}

This distinction is the foundation of the entire sizing procedure.

2.2 Speed-Dependent Lift-to-Drag Ratio

The lift-to-drag ratio of an Inductrack-type system is not constant. At low speeds, the induced currents in the track are far from optimal, and the L/D ratio is significantly lower than the high-speed asymptotic value. Literature-derived ranges are:

\left(\frac{L}{D}\right)_{\rm Inductrack\ I} \approx 15\text{–}30 \quad \text{at } v \approx 8\ \text{m/s},

\left(\frac{L}{D}\right)_{\rm Inductrack\ II} \approx 30\text{–}60 \quad \text{at } v \approx 8\ \text{m/s}.

These ranges are theoretical estimates derived from the published Inductrack literature and are not measured values for the reference vehicle. They define the uncertainty band within which the load model must be evaluated.

2.3 Magnetic Drag Force and Power

For a vehicle of mass m and weight W = mg, the magnetic drag force at lift-to-drag ratio L/D is:

F_D = \frac{W}{L/D} = \frac{mg}{L/D}.

For m = 500 kg, g = 9.81 m/s², and L/D in the range 15–60:

L/D F_D (N) P_D = F_D v at 8 m/s (kW)
15 327 2.62
30 163.5 1.31
60 81.75 0.65

The mechanical drag power at cruise speed is therefore in the range 0.65–2.62 kW, with a central estimate of approximately 1.3 kW for L/D = 30.

2.4 Aerodynamic Drag

Aerodynamic drag at urban speeds is small but not negligible:

F_{\rm aero} = \frac{1}{2} \rho C_d A v^2.

For \rho = 1.225 kg/m³, C_d = 0.3, A = 2 m², v = 8 m/s:

F_{\rm aero} = \frac{1}{2} \cdot 1.225 \cdot 0.3 \cdot 2 \cdot 64 = 23.5\ \text{N}.

The corresponding aerodynamic power is P_{\rm aero} = 23.5 \times 8 = 188 W. This is small compared to the magnetic drag but is included for completeness.

2.5 Propulsion Power per Vehicle

The linear motor must supply the total thrust F_{\rm prop} = F_D + F_{\rm aero}. The electrical power drawn from the road is:

P_{\rm prop} = \frac{F_{\rm prop} \cdot v}{\eta_{\rm LIM}},

where \eta_{\rm LIM} is the linear-motor efficiency. Literature-derived LIM efficiencies for urban transit range from 0.47 for a compact 1.6 m motor at 40 km/h to 0.85 for a long (10 m) LIM at the same speed. A central estimate of \eta_{\rm LIM} = 0.70 is adopted for the reference corridor, with the uncertainty range 0.50–0.85.

Central estimate (L/D = 30, \eta_{\rm LIM} = 0.70):

F_{\rm prop} = 163.5 + 23.5 = 187\ \text{N},

P_{\rm prop} = \frac{187 \times 8}{0.70} = 2.14\ \text{kW}.

Uncertainty band (L/D = 15–60, \eta_{\rm LIM} = 0.50\text{–}0.85):

P_{\rm prop} \in [1.05,\ 7.00]\ \text{kW}.

This is an order-of-magnitude band, not a precise value. It is significantly higher than the 0.6 kW assumption of previous revisions and significantly lower than the 26.7 kW estimate obtained from a 40 W/kg drag assumption, which is not supported by the low-speed L/D data.

2.6 Electrical Load per Vehicle

Item Symbol Central estimate Uncertainty band
Propulsion (linear motor) P_{\rm prop} 2.14 kW 1.05–7.00 kW
Onboard auxiliaries P_{\rm aux} 1.0 kW 0.5–2.0 kW
Control and communication P_{\rm ctrl} 0.2 kW 0.1–0.5 kW
Total per vehicle P_{\rm veh} 3.34 kW 1.65–9.50 kW

The auxiliary and control loads are stated as design targets, not measured values. They must be validated by vehicle specification in a future programme.

2.7 Electrical Load per Corridor

For N_{\rm veh} = 20 simultaneous vehicles on 1 km:

P_{\rm corridor} = N_{\rm veh} \times P_{\rm veh}.

Central estimate: P_{\rm corridor} = 20 \times 3.34 = 66.8 kW.

With distribution efficiency \eta_{\rm dist} = 0.92 and a 50% design margin:

P_{\rm station}^{\rm design} = \frac{66.8}{0.92} \times 1.5 \approx 109\ \text{kW} \approx 0.11\ \text{MVA}.

Upper bound (using P_{\rm veh} = 9.50 kW):

P_{\rm station}^{\rm design} \approx 0.31\ \text{MVA}.

The reference substation rating is therefore taken as 0.25–0.50 MVA, covering the central estimate and the upper bound.

2.8 Architecture Selection

The thesis cannot simultaneously claim passive PMEDS (no ground propulsion) and ground linear-motor propulsion. Three architectures are possible:

Architecture Road supplies Vehicle carries
A. Onboard propulsion Sensors, communication, signaling, lighting Battery + propulsion motor
B. Ground propulsion All propulsion + infrastructure Small auxiliary battery
C. Hybrid Propulsion on equipped corridors Battery for off-corridor and degraded modes

Because the thesis title specifies PMEDS (passive levitation), and because passive levitation at low speed requires either wheels or a separate propulsion system, Architecture C is adopted as the baseline. The road supplies propulsion on equipped corridors via short-stator linear motors; the vehicle carries a battery for off-corridor operation and for degraded modes. Architecture B is retained as a limiting case for corridors where full ground propulsion is available.

2.9 Falsification Criterion

The load model is falsified if electromagnetic simulation or measurement shows that the low-speed L/D ratio lies outside the band 15–60, or if the LIM efficiency lies outside 0.47–0.85, or if the auxiliary and control loads differ from the stated design targets by more than 50%.

