5. Cable Sizing

5.1 Loop Resistance

For a copper conductor of length L (one way) and cross-section A, the loop resistance (there and back) is:

R_{\rm loop} = 2\frac{\rho(T)L}{A},

with \rho(90°C) = 2.2 \times 10^{-8} Ω·m for copper.

5.2 Voltage Drop and Power Loss

\Delta V = I R_{\rm loop} = \frac{P_{\rm segment}}{V_{\rm DC}} \cdot \frac{2\rho L}{A},

P_{\rm loss} = I^2 R_{\rm loop} = \frac{P_{\rm segment}^2}{V_{\rm DC}^2} \cdot \frac{2\rho L}{A}.

The voltage drop must satisfy \Delta V / V_{\rm DC} \le \epsilon_{\max}. For DC systems with inverters, the admissible regulation at inverter terminals is < 10%, not 5% as for AC traction. The reference value is \epsilon_{\max} = 0.05 (5%) for margin.

5.3 IEC 60287 Ampacity for Buried Cables

The 3 A/mm² current density is valid for cables in free air. For buried DC traction cables, the ampacity is governed by IEC 60287 and depends on:

1. Soil thermal resistivity \rho_{\rm soil}, ranging from 0.5 to 1.2 °C·m/W under loading conditions; typical urban backfill (dry sand) is 2.5 K·m/W.
2. Burial depth and grouping factor for positive and negative conductors in the same trench.
3. Load factor and ambient temperature.

For typical urban conditions (dry sand, \rho_{\rm soil} \approx 2.5 K·m/W, both conductors in the same trench), the derating factor is f_{\rm soil} \approx 0.70. The effective current density for buried DC traction cables is therefore:

J_{\rm eff} = J_{\rm air} \cdot f_{\rm soil} \approx 3 \times 0.70 = 2.1\ \text{A/mm}^2.

A more conservative value of 1.5 A/mm² is adopted for the reference corridor, consistent with IEC 60287 practice for buried cables in poor soil.

5.4 Harmonic Loss Factor

The 12-pulse rectifier produces characteristic harmonics (11th, 13th, etc.). The harmonic loss factor for a 12-pulse rectifier is f_{\rm harm} \approx 0.98, meaning the RMS current is approximately 2% higher than the DC current for the same fundamental power. This is included as a small correction.

5.5 Sizing Procedure

1. Choose V_{\rm DC} = 3000 V.
2. Choose \epsilon_{\max} = 0.05.
3. Compute the voltage-drop cross-section:

A_{\rm drop} \ge \frac{2\rho L P_{\rm segment}}{V_{\rm DC}^2 \epsilon_{\max}}.

4. Compute the thermal cross-section using the derated current density:

A_{\rm thermal} \ge \frac{P_{\rm segment}}{V_{\rm DC} J_{\rm eff} f_{\rm harm}}.

5. Select A = \max(A_{\rm drop}, A_{\rm thermal}) and round up to the next standard size.

5.6 Numerical Example

For P_{\rm segment} = 0.5 MW, V_{\rm DC} = 3000 V, L = 250 m, \epsilon_{\max} = 0.05:

A_{\rm drop} \ge \frac{2 \cdot 2.2 \times 10^{-8} \cdot 250 \cdot 5 \times 10^5}{9 \times 10^6 \cdot 0.05} = 12.2\ \text{mm}^2.

The current is I = 5 \times 10^5 / 3000 = 167 A. With J_{\rm eff} = 1.5 A/mm² and f_{\rm harm} = 0.98:

A_{\rm thermal} \ge \frac{167}{1.5 \times 0.98} = 113.6\ \text{mm}^2.

The thermal constraint dominates. The next standard size is 120 mm².

