To avoid the circularity of scaling A with P, the table below uses a fixed standard cross-section of 120 mm² and varies the segment power:

P_{\rm segment} I R_{\rm loop}/250 m Loss \Delta V \Delta V/V
100 kW 33.3 A 0.092 Ω 102 W 3.1 V 0.10%
250 kW 83.3 A 0.092 Ω 638 W 7.7 V 0.26%
500 kW 167 A 0.092 Ω 2.56 kW 15.3 V 0.51%
1 MW 333 A 0.092 Ω 10.2 kW 30.7 V 1.02%

At 1 MW, the 120 mm² cable carries 333 A, which exceeds the derated ampacity of 120 \times 1.5 \times 0.98 = 176 A. The cable must therefore be upsized to 250 mm² for 1 MW operation. The table demonstrates that the voltage drop is small even at 1 MW; the thermal limit is the binding constraint.

5.8 Return Path and Stray Current

Under a public road, the return conductor design affects stray currents, corrosion, EMI, and safety. Traction negative is often left ungrounded to limit stray current, requiring insulation monitoring devices (IMD) per IEC 61557. The reference corridor adopts:

1. Floating or impedance-grounded DC system.
2. Insulation monitoring with continuous resistance measurement.
3. Return conductor in the same trench as the positive conductor, with a separation that minimizes loop inductance.

