7. Storage and Regenerative Braking

7.1 Regenerative Braking Energy

The kinetic energy of a 500 kg vehicle at 8 m/s is:

E_{\rm kin} = \frac{1}{2} m v^2 = \frac{1}{2} \cdot 500 \cdot 64 = 16\ \text{kJ}.

With recovery efficiency \eta_{\rm reg} = 0.70, recoverable energy is 11.2 kJ per stop. For 20 simultaneous vehicles stopping, total recoverable energy is 224 kJ = 0.062 kWh. This is small compared to the substation rating but is a power problem, not an energy problem.

7.2 Regenerative Braking Power

If 20 vehicles brake simultaneously in T_{\rm brake} = 2 s, the reverse power is:

P_{\rm reg} = \frac{224\ \text{kJ}}{2\ \text{s}} = 112\ \text{kW}.

This reverse power must be absorbed or dissipated. A 12-pulse diode rectifier is non-reversible. The reference corridor specifies active (IGBT) rectifiers for reversibility. If a diode rectifier is retained, a braking resistor or reversible converter is required.

7.3 Storage Sizing: Energy and Power Constraints

Theorem 7.1 (Minimum storage energy). The minimum supercapacitor energy required to cover a transient of duration T_{\rm transient} and amplitude \Delta P = P_{\rm peak} - P_{\rm avg} is:

\boxed{E_{\rm SC} \ge \frac{\Delta P \cdot T_{\rm transient}}{\eta_{\rm SC}}},

where \eta_{\rm SC} is the supercapacitor round-trip efficiency (typically 0.95).

Proof. During the transient, the supercapacitor must supply \Delta P for duration T_{\rm transient}. The stored energy must exceed the delivered energy divided by the round-trip efficiency. ∎

Theorem 7.2 (Minimum storage power). The supercapacitor converter must satisfy:

\boxed{P_{\rm SC} \ge \Delta P}.

Proof. The supercapacitor must deliver the full transient power at the instant of peak demand. The converter rating is therefore at least \Delta P. ∎

For \Delta P = 50 kW and T_{\rm transient} = 2 s:

E_{\rm SC} \ge \frac{50 \cdot 2}{0.95} \approx 105\ \text{kJ} \approx 0.03\ \text{kWh},

P_{\rm SC} \ge 50\ \text{kW}.

For a 1 km corridor with 4 substations, total supercapacitor storage is ~0.12 kWh, with 200 kW total converter rating.

7.4 Critical Load Decomposition

Item Peak power Duration Continuous power
Gear actuation 5.0 kW 1 s 0.5 kW (holding)
Sensors 0.5 kW — 0.5 kW
Communication 0.2 kW — 0.2 kW
Emergency lighting 0.3 kW — 0.3 kW
Total 6.0 kW 1 s 1.5 kW

Theorem 7.3 (Minimum battery energy). The battery must supply the critical load for the ride-through time T_{\rm ride}:

\boxed{E_{\rm bat} \ge P_{\rm crit}^{\rm peak} \cdot t_{\rm peak} + P_{\rm crit}^{\rm cont} \cdot T_{\rm ride}}.

Proof. During the ride-through, the battery must supply the peak critical load P_{\rm crit}^{\rm peak} for duration t_{\rm peak} and the continuous critical load P_{\rm crit}^{\rm cont} for the remaining duration T_{\rm ride} - t_{\rm peak}. Energy conservation gives the result. ∎

For P_{\rm crit}^{\rm peak} = 6 kW, t_{\rm peak} = 1 s, P_{\rm crit}^{\rm cont} = 1.5 kW, T_{\rm ride} = 30 s:

E_{\rm bat} \ge 6 \cdot 1 + 1.5 \cdot 30 = 51\ \text{kJ} \approx 0.014\ \text{kWh}.

With a 50% depth-of-discharge limit and a 2× margin, the onboard battery should be 0.05 kWh. A 1 kWh onboard LiFePO₄ battery provides a 20× margin.

