8. Resilience and Discriminating Kernel

8.1 Power Supply State

Let q_{\rm elec} \in \{0, 1, 2\} denote the electrical state:

· q_{\rm elec} = 0: nominal (both sources available).
· q_{\rm elec} = 1: degraded (one source available).
· q_{\rm elec} = 2: loss of all sources.

8.2 Discriminating Kernel

The electrical discriminating kernel is:

\boxed{
\operatorname{Disc}_{\rm elec} = \{x_0 : \exists u(\cdot), \forall d_{\rm elec}(\cdot), \exists T \ge 0, x(T) \in \mathcal{C}_{\rm safe}, x(t) \in \mathcal{K} \ \forall t \in [0, T]\}
}

where d_{\rm elec} is the electrical disturbance (source loss, converter failure), \mathcal{C}_{\rm safe} is the safe-stop set, and \mathcal{K} is the operational envelope.

8.3 Admissibility

The corridor is admissible if:

\boxed{\mathcal{K}_{\rm operating} \subseteq \operatorname{Disc}_{\rm elec}(\mathcal{K}, \mathcal{C}_{\rm safe}).}

8.4 Voltage Continuity

For the electrical state to remain within \mathcal{K}, the DC bus voltage V_{\rm DC}(t) must satisfy:

V_{\min} \le V_{\rm DC}(t) \le V_{\max}, \qquad \forall t.

Theorem 8.1 (Voltage stability condition). Under a step load increase \Delta P at constant generation P_{\rm gen}, the DC bus voltage satisfies:

C_{\rm bus} \frac{dV_{\rm DC}}{dt} = \frac{P_{\rm gen} - P_{\rm load}}{V_{\rm DC}},

which has the solution:

V_{\rm DC}(t) = \sqrt{V_0^2 - \frac{2 \Delta P \cdot t}{C_{\rm bus}}}.

The bus capacitance must satisfy:

C_{\rm bus} \ge \frac{2 \Delta P \cdot T_{\rm ride}}{V_0^2 - V_{\min}^2}

for the voltage to remain above V_{\min} for the entire ride-through time.

Proof. Separating variables and integrating from V_0 to V_{\rm DC}(t):

\int_{V_0}^{V_{\rm DC}} V dV = \int_0^t \frac{P_{\rm gen} - P_{\rm load}}{C_{\rm bus}} dt \implies \frac{V_{\rm DC}^2 - V_0^2}{2} = -\frac{\Delta P \cdot t}{C_{\rm bus}}.

Rearranging gives the result. The minimum capacitance follows by setting V_{\rm DC}(T_{\rm ride}) = V_{\min}. ∎

For \Delta P = 100 kW, T_{\rm ride} = 100 ms, V_0 = 3000 V, V_{\min} = 2700 V:

C_{\rm bus} \ge \frac{2 \cdot 100\,000 \cdot 0.1}{9 \times 10^6 - 7.29 \times 10^6} = \frac{20\,000}{1.71 \times 10^6} = 0.0117\ \text{F}.

A 12 mF bus capacitor per substation is a modest requirement.

8.5 Computation of the Discriminating Kernel

State space. The electrical state is x = (q_{\rm elec}, V_{\rm DC}, SOC_{\rm bat}). The safe set is:

\mathcal{C}_{\rm safe} = \{(q, V, SOC) : V \ge V_{\min},\ SOC \ge SOC_{\min}\}.

The operational envelope is:

\mathcal{K} = \{(q, V, SOC) : q \in \{0,1,2\},\ V_{\min} \le V \le V_{\max},\ SOC_{\min} \le SOC \le SOC_{\max}\}.

Disturbance model. The disturbance d_{\rm elec} is a sequence of source losses. The worst case is the loss of all sources at t=0, i.e., q_{\rm elec}(0) = 2.

Under-approximation. Using the ellipsoid/support-vector method for parametric approximations of viability kernels, the discriminating kernel is under-approximated by:

\operatorname{Disc}_{\rm elec}^{\rm under} = \left\{ (q, V, SOC) : V \ge V_{\min} + \Delta V_{\rm decay}(T_{\rm ride}),\ SOC \ge SOC_{\min} + \frac{P_{\rm crit}^{\rm cont} \cdot T_{\rm ride}}{E_{\rm bat}} \right\},

where \Delta V_{\rm decay}(T_{\rm ride}) is obtained from Theorem 8.1.

Numerical evaluation. For the reference corridor:

· V_{\min} = 2700 V, V_{\max} = 3300 V.
· SOC_{\min} = 0.2, SOC_{\max} = 0.9.
· C_{\rm bus} = 12 mF, \Delta P = 100 kW, T_{\rm ride} = 100 ms.

From Theorem 8.1:

\Delta V_{\rm decay}(100\ \text{ms}) = 3000 - \sqrt{9 \times 10^6 - \frac{2 \cdot 100\,000 \cdot 0.1}{0.012}} = 3000 - 2700 = 300\ \text{V}.

The under-approximated kernel is therefore:

\operatorname{Disc}_{\rm elec}^{\rm under} = \{(q, V, SOC) : V \ge 3000\ \text{V},\ SOC \ge 0.2 + 0.014\} = \{(q, V, SOC) : V \ge 3000\ \text{V},\ SOC \ge 0.214\}.

Conclusion. The discriminating kernel is non-empty for the reference corridor. The admissibility condition \mathcal{K}_{\rm operating} \subseteq \operatorname{Disc}_{\rm elec} is satisfied provided that:

1. Each substation has N−1 redundancy (two independent AC feeds).
2. The bus capacitance satisfies C_{\rm bus} \ge 12 mF.
3. The onboard battery has SOC \ge 0.214 at the start of any ride-through.
4. The ride-through time T_{\rm ride} is consistent with the braking-distance analysis (Chapter 6).

