10. Gated Theoretical Research Programme

10.1 Gates

Gate 0 — Load model. Establish the theoretical electrical load profile per vehicle and per corridor. Perform electromagnetic simulation of the exact Halbach + track geometry to determine L/D(v) in the range 0–16 m/s. Derive P_{\rm prop}(v), P_{\rm drag, mag}(v), P_{\rm aero}(v). Validate the LIM efficiency against manufacturer data.

Gate 1 — Substation sizing. Compute nominal rating and overload for the reference corridor under N−1 contingency for N=2, 4, 5. Verify the assumptions of Theorem 3.1 by load-flow simulation.

Gate 2 — DC bus selection. Compute the optimal DC voltage using the scalar cost model. Assess sensitivity to \kappa_c and \kappa_v. Obtain supplier quotes for 1500 V and 3000 V converters.

Gate 3 — Cable sizing. Compute cross-section, loop resistance, loss, and voltage drop for each segment using IEC 60287 ampacity. Include soil thermal derating, harmonic loss, and return-path design.

Gate 4 — Protection coordination. Simulate fault scenarios: source loss, converter failure, cable fault. Verify selectivity and ride-through. Verify that the N−1 overload transient does not trip protection.

Gate 5 — Storage sizing. Compute supercapacitor and battery requirements from both energy and power constraints. Include regenerative braking reversibility.

Gate 6 — Thermal analysis. Model track heating under continuous magnetic drag. Verify that the track temperature remains within structural limits.

Gate 7 — Resilience. Compute the electrical discriminating kernel using the ellipsoid/support-vector method for the reduced-order model.

Gate 8 — Corridor demonstrator (1 km). Integrate all models in a hardware-in-the-loop or scale-model simulation. Validate the power + protection + SIAB chain before any physical construction.

Gate 9 — Network generalization. Extend to a 10 km urban network with intersections and alternative routes.

Gate 10 — Techno-economic analysis. Compute cost per km as a function of substation spacing, DC voltage, and redundancy configuration. Identify the economic optimum.

10.2 Falsification Criteria

· Low-speed L/D ratio outside the band 15–60.
· LIM efficiency outside the band 0.47–0.85.
· Auxiliary and control loads differ from design targets by more than 50%.
· Voltage drop under nominal load > 5%.
· Voltage drop under fault > 10%.
· Overload under N−1 > 150% for 60 s.
· Fault detection time > 3 ms.
· Ride-through time < 30 s.
· Insulation resistance < 1 MΩ.
· Discriminating kernel empty.
· Cable ampacity (derated) below 1.5 A/mm².
· Regenerative peak power exceeds rectifier reverse capability.
· Cost per km exceeds the techno-economic target by more than 50%.

