Appendix B: Proof of the DC Bus Optimality

Theorem B.1 (Scalar cost optimum). Under the scalar cost model:

C(V) = \kappa_c \frac{P}{VJ} L + \kappa_v V,

the optimal DC voltage is:

V^* = \sqrt{\frac{\kappa_c P L}{\kappa_v J}}.

Proof. The total cost is the sum of cable cost and converter cost:

C(V) = C_{\rm cable}(V) + C_{\rm conv}(V).

The cable cross-section for current I = P/V at current density J is:

A = \frac{I}{J} = \frac{P}{VJ}.

The cable cost per metre is c_c = \kappa_c A, so the total cable cost over length L is:

C_{\rm cable}(V) = \kappa_c A L = \kappa_c \frac{P}{VJ} L.

The converter cost is assumed linear in voltage:

C_{\rm conv}(V) = \kappa_v V.

Differentiating the total cost with respect to V:

\frac{dC}{dV} = -\frac{\kappa_c P L}{J V^2} + \kappa_v.

Setting \frac{dC}{dV} = 0:

\frac{\kappa_c P L}{J V^2} = \kappa_v \implies V^2 = \frac{\kappa_c P L}{\kappa_v J} \implies V^* = \sqrt{\frac{\kappa_c P L}{\kappa_v J}}.

The second derivative is:

\frac{d^2 C}{dV^2} = \frac{2 \kappa_c P L}{J V^3} > 0 \quad \forall V > 0,

confirming that V^* is a minimum. ∎

Proposition B.2 (Sensitivity). The sensitivity of V^* to the cost coefficients is:

\frac{\partial V^*}{\partial \kappa_c} = \frac{1}{2} \sqrt{\frac{P L}{\kappa_c \kappa_v J}} > 0,

\frac{\partial V^*}{\partial \kappa_v} = -\frac{1}{2} \sqrt{\frac{\kappa_c P L}{\kappa_v^3 J}} < 0,

\frac{\partial V^*}{\partial J} = -\frac{1}{2} \sqrt{\frac{\kappa_c P L}{\kappa_v J^3}} < 0.

The optimal voltage increases with cable cost and decreases with converter cost and current density.

Proposition B.3 (Non-linearity correction). If the converter cost is non-linear, C_{\rm conv}(V) = \kappa_v V^\alpha with \alpha > 1, the optimal voltage becomes:

V^* = \left( \frac{\kappa_c P L}{\alpha \kappa_v J} \right)^{1/(\alpha+1)}.

For \alpha = 2 (quadratic converter cost):

V^* = \left( \frac{\kappa_c P L}{2 \kappa_v J} \right)^{1/3}.

For P = 0.5 MW, L = 250 m, J = 1.5 A/mm², \kappa_c = 10 USD/(mm²·m), \kappa_v = 100 USD/kV²:

V^* = \left( \frac{10 \cdot 5 \times 10^5 \cdot 250}{2 \cdot 100 \cdot 1.5} \right)^{1/3} = \left( 4.17 \times 10^6 \right)^{1/3} \approx 1.61\ \text{kV}.

This demonstrates that the optimal voltage is highly sensitive to the assumed converter cost model. The practical choice of 3000 V is therefore justified by standardization, not by the scalar model.

