Appendix C: Proof of the Cable Sizing Conditions

Theorem C.1 (Voltage-drop cross-section). The minimum cross-section for a segment of power P_{\rm segment} and length L at DC voltage V_{\rm DC} with voltage-drop constraint \epsilon_{\max} is:

A_{\rm drop} \ge \frac{2\rho L P_{\rm segment}}{V_{\rm DC}^2 \epsilon_{\max}}.

Proof. The loop resistance for a one-way length L and cross-section A is:

R_{\rm loop} = \frac{2\rho L}{A}.

The voltage drop is:

\Delta V = I R_{\rm loop} = \frac{P_{\rm segment}}{V_{\rm DC}} \cdot \frac{2\rho L}{A}.

The constraint is \Delta V \le \epsilon_{\max} V_{\rm DC}. Substituting:

\frac{P_{\rm segment}}{V_{\rm DC}} \cdot \frac{2\rho L}{A} \le \epsilon_{\max} V_{\rm DC}.

Multiplying both sides by A:

\frac{2\rho L P_{\rm segment}}{V_{\rm DC}} \le \epsilon_{\max} V_{\rm DC} A.

Dividing by \epsilon_{\max} V_{\rm DC}:

A \ge \frac{2\rho L P_{\rm segment}}{V_{\rm DC}^2 \epsilon_{\max}}.

∎

Theorem C.2 (Thermal cross-section). The minimum cross-section for a segment of power P_{\rm segment} at DC voltage V_{\rm DC} with derated current density J_{\rm eff} and harmonic factor f_{\rm harm} is:

A_{\rm thermal} \ge \frac{P_{\rm segment}}{V_{\rm DC} J_{\rm eff} f_{\rm harm}}.

Proof. The RMS current including harmonics is:

I_{\rm rms} = \frac{I_{\rm DC}}{f_{\rm harm}} = \frac{P_{\rm segment}}{V_{\rm DC} f_{\rm harm}}.

The cross-section required to keep the current density below J_{\rm eff} is:

A_{\rm thermal} \ge \frac{I_{\rm rms}}{J_{\rm eff}} = \frac{P_{\rm segment}}{V_{\rm DC} J_{\rm eff} f_{\rm harm}}.

∎

Theorem C.3 (Combined sizing rule). The required cross-section is:

A = \max\left( A_{\rm drop}, A_{\rm thermal} \right),

rounded up to the next standard cable size.

Proof. Both constraints must be satisfied simultaneously. The cross-section must be at least A_{\rm drop} to satisfy the voltage-drop constraint and at least A_{\rm thermal} to satisfy the thermal constraint. Therefore A \ge \max(A_{\rm drop}, A_{\rm thermal}). ∎

Corollary C.4 (Dominance of thermal constraint). The thermal constraint dominates when:

\frac{P_{\rm segment}}{V_{\rm DC} J_{\rm eff} f_{\rm harm}} > \frac{2\rho L P_{\rm segment}}{V_{\rm DC}^2 \epsilon_{\max}}.

Simplifying:

\frac{1}{J_{\rm eff} f_{\rm harm}} > \frac{2\rho L}{V_{\rm DC} \epsilon_{\max}}.

For J_{\rm eff} = 1.5 A/mm², f_{\rm harm} = 0.98, \rho = 2.2 \times 10^{-8} Ω·m, L = 250 m, V_{\rm DC} = 3000 V, \epsilon_{\max} = 0.05:

\frac{1}{1.5 \cdot 0.98} = 0.68 > \frac{2 \cdot 2.2 \times 10^{-8} \cdot 250}{3000 \cdot 0.05} = 7.33 \times 10^{-5}.

The inequality is satisfied by four orders of magnitude, confirming that the thermal constraint dominates for the reference corridor parameters.

