Appendix D: Proof of the Voltage Stability Condition

Theorem D.1 (DC bus capacitance bound). Under a step load increase \Delta P at constant generation, the DC bus capacitance must satisfy:

C_{\rm bus} \ge \frac{2 \Delta P \cdot T_{\rm ride}}{V_0^2 - V_{\min}^2}

for the voltage to remain above V_{\min} for the entire ride-through time T_{\rm ride}.

Proof. The DC bus power balance is:

P_{\rm gen} - P_{\rm load} = V_{\rm DC} I_{\rm DC} = V_{\rm DC} \cdot C_{\rm bus} \frac{dV_{\rm DC}}{dt}.

Rearranging:

C_{\rm bus} V_{\rm DC} \frac{dV_{\rm DC}}{dt} = P_{\rm gen} - P_{\rm load}.

Under a step load increase \Delta P = P_{\rm load} - P_{\rm gen}:

C_{\rm bus} V_{\rm DC} \frac{dV_{\rm DC}}{dt} = -\Delta P.

Separating variables:

V_{\rm DC} \, dV_{\rm DC} = -\frac{\Delta P}{C_{\rm bus}} dt.

Integrating from V_0 at t=0 to V_{\rm DC}(t) at time t:

\int_{V_0}^{V_{\rm DC}(t)} V \, dV = -\frac{\Delta P}{C_{\rm bus}} \int_0^t dt.

\frac{V_{\rm DC}^2(t) - V_0^2}{2} = -\frac{\Delta P \cdot t}{C_{\rm bus}}.

Solving for V_{\rm DC}(t):

V_{\rm DC}(t) = \sqrt{V_0^2 - \frac{2 \Delta P \cdot t}{C_{\rm bus}}}.

The voltage remains above V_{\min} for all t \in [0, T_{\rm ride}] if and only if:

V_{\rm DC}(T_{\rm ride}) \ge V_{\min}.

Substituting:

\sqrt{V_0^2 - \frac{2 \Delta P \cdot T_{\rm ride}}{C_{\rm bus}}} \ge V_{\min}.

Squaring both sides:

V_0^2 - \frac{2 \Delta P \cdot T_{\rm ride}}{C_{\rm bus}} \ge V_{\min}^2.

Rearranging:

\frac{2 \Delta P \cdot T_{\rm ride}}{C_{\rm bus}} \le V_0^2 - V_{\min}^2.

Therefore:

C_{\rm bus} \ge \frac{2 \Delta P \cdot T_{\rm ride}}{V_0^2 - V_{\min}^2}.

∎

Corollary D.2 (Numerical value). For \Delta P = 100 kW, T_{\rm ride} = 100 ms, V_0 = 3000 V, V_{\min} = 2700 V:

C_{\rm bus} \ge \frac{2 \cdot 100\,000 \cdot 0.1}{3000^2 - 2700^2} = \frac{20\,000}{9\,000\,000 - 7\,290\,000} = \frac{20\,000}{1\,710\,000} = 0.0117\ \text{F} = 11.7\ \text{mF}.

Corollary D.3 (Energy interpretation). The energy stored in the bus capacitance at V_0 is:

E_{\rm bus} = \frac{1}{2} C_{\rm bus} V_0^2 = \frac{1}{2} \cdot 0.0117 \cdot 9 \times 10^6 = 52\,650\ \text{J} = 52.65\ \text{kJ}.

The energy delivered during the ride-through is \Delta P \cdot T_{\rm ride} = 100\,000 \cdot 0.1 = 10\,000 J = 10 kJ. The bus capacitance therefore provides a 5.3× margin over the required ride-through energy, which accounts for the fact that the voltage is allowed to drop from 3000 V to 2700 V, extracting only part of the stored energy.

