Appendix E: Proof of the Storage Sizing Theorems

Theorem E.1 (Minimum supercapacitor energy). The minimum supercapacitor energy required to cover a transient of duration T_{\rm transient} and amplitude \Delta P is:

E_{\rm SC} \ge \frac{\Delta P \cdot T_{\rm transient}}{\eta_{\rm SC}},

where \eta_{\rm SC} is the supercapacitor round-trip efficiency.

Proof. During the transient, the supercapacitor must deliver power \Delta P for duration T_{\rm transient}. The delivered energy is:

E_{\rm delivered} = \Delta P \cdot T_{\rm transient}.

The stored energy E_{\rm SC} is related to the delivered energy by the round-trip efficiency:

E_{\rm delivered} = \eta_{\rm SC} E_{\rm SC}.

Therefore:

E_{\rm SC} = \frac{E_{\rm delivered}}{\eta_{\rm SC}} = \frac{\Delta P \cdot T_{\rm transient}}{\eta_{\rm SC}}.

The inequality E_{\rm SC} \ge \Delta P \cdot T_{\rm transient} / \eta_{\rm SC} follows from the requirement that the stored energy must be at least the minimum required. ∎

Theorem E.2 (Minimum supercapacitor power). The supercapacitor converter must satisfy:

P_{\rm SC} \ge \Delta P.

Proof. The supercapacitor must deliver the full transient power \Delta P at the instant of peak demand. The converter rating must therefore be at least \Delta P. ∎

Theorem E.3 (Minimum battery energy). The battery must supply the critical load for the ride-through time T_{\rm ride}:

E_{\rm bat} \ge P_{\rm crit}^{\rm peak} \cdot t_{\rm peak} + P_{\rm crit}^{\rm cont} \cdot (T_{\rm ride} - t_{\rm peak}).

Proof. During the ride-through, the battery must supply:

1. The peak critical load P_{\rm crit}^{\rm peak} for duration t_{\rm peak}.
2. The continuous critical load P_{\rm crit}^{\rm cont} for the remaining duration T_{\rm ride} - t_{\rm peak}.

The total energy required is:

E_{\rm bat} = P_{\rm crit}^{\rm peak} \cdot t_{\rm peak} + P_{\rm crit}^{\rm cont} \cdot (T_{\rm ride} - t_{\rm peak}).

The inequality follows from the requirement that the battery energy must be at least the required energy. ∎

Corollary E.4 (Numerical values). For \Delta P = 50 kW, T_{\rm transient} = 2 s, \eta_{\rm SC} = 0.95:

E_{\rm SC} \ge \frac{50\,000 \cdot 2}{0.95} = 105\,263\ \text{J} \approx 0.029\ \text{kWh},

P_{\rm SC} \ge 50\ \text{kW}.

For P_{\rm crit}^{\rm peak} = 6 kW, t_{\rm peak} = 1 s, P_{\rm crit}^{\rm cont} = 1.5 kW, T_{\rm ride} = 30 s:

E_{\rm bat} \ge 6\,000 \cdot 1 + 1\,500 \cdot 29 = 6\,000 + 43\,500 = 49\,500\ \text{J} \approx 0.014\ \text{kWh}.

With a 50% depth-of-discharge limit and a 2× margin:

E_{\rm bat}^{\rm design} = \frac{0.014}{0.5} \times 2 = 0.056\ \text{kWh} \approx 0.05\ \text{kWh}.

A 1 kWh onboard battery provides a 20× margin over the design value.

