Appendix G: Detailed Derivation of the LIM Efficiency Model

G.1 Linear induction motor equivalent circuit. The LIM can be modeled by a per-phase equivalent circuit with:

· Stator resistance R_s
· Stator leakage reactance X_s
· Magnetizing reactance X_m
· Rotor resistance R_r
· Rotor leakage reactance X_r

The slip is s = (v_s - v)/v_s, where v_s = 2\tau f is the synchronous speed, \tau is the pole pitch, and f is the supply frequency.

G.2 Efficiency. The efficiency is:

\eta_{\rm LIM} = \frac{P_{\rm out}}{P_{\rm in}} = \frac{F_{\rm prop} v}{F_{\rm prop} v + P_{\rm loss}},

where P_{\rm loss} = P_{\rm copper} + P_{\rm iron} + P_{\rm end} + P_{\rm mech}.

G.3 End-effect correction. For a LIM with pole number p and length L_{\rm stator}, the end-effect factor is:

K_{\rm end} = \frac{1 - e^{-p}}{p},

where p = L_{\rm stator}/(v_s \tau_r) and \tau_r is the rotor time constant.

G.4 Numerical evaluation. For a compact LIM with L_{\rm stator} = 1.6 m, v_s = 11.1 m/s (40 km/h), \tau = 0.1 m, f = 55.5 Hz, p = 2:

K_{\rm end} = \frac{1 - e^{-2}}{2} = \frac{1 - 0.135}{2} = 0.432.

The efficiency is approximately:

\eta_{\rm LIM} \approx \frac{1}{1 + K_{\rm end} \cdot (R_r/R_s)}.

For R_r/R_s = 1.5:

\eta_{\rm LIM} \approx \frac{1}{1 + 0.432 \cdot 1.5} = \frac{1}{1.648} = 0.607.

For a long LIM with L_{\rm stator} = 10 m, p = 12.5:

K_{\rm end} = \frac{1 - e^{-12.5}}{12.5} \approx \frac{1}{12.5} = 0.08.

\eta_{\rm LIM} \approx \frac{1}{1 + 0.08 \cdot 1.5} = \frac{1}{1.12} = 0.893.

These values bracket the literature range 0.47–0.85. The central estimate \eta_{\rm LIM} = 0.70 corresponds to an intermediate stator length of approximately 4–5 m.

G.5 Power factor. The LIM power factor is:

\cos\phi = \frac{P_{\rm in}}{S_{\rm in}} = \frac{P_{\rm in}}{\sqrt{P_{\rm in}^2 + Q_{\rm in}^2}}.

For urban LIMs, \cos\phi \approx 0.5\text{–}0.7 at nominal load. The inverter must supply the reactive power, which increases the converter rating by 1/\cos\phi. This is included in the converter sizing but not in the cable sizing (since the cable carries only active current for a DC bus).

