Appendix I: Braking Distance Analysis Details

I.1 Stopping distance. The stopping distance is the sum of the reaction distance, the gear deployment distance, and the braking distance:

d_{\rm stop}(v) = v \cdot t_{\rm detect} + v \cdot t_{\rm gear} + \frac{v^2}{2 a_{\rm brake}}.

I.2 Braking deceleration. The braking deceleration is limited by the friction coefficient \mu between the wheels and the road:

a_{\rm brake} = \mu g.

For dry asphalt, \mu \approx 0.7\text{–}0.9; for wet asphalt, \mu \approx 0.4\text{–}0.6. The reference value a_{\rm brake} = 4 m/s² corresponds to \mu = 0.41, a conservative value for wet conditions.

I.3 Gear deployment time. The gear deployment time t_{\rm gear} is the time required to extend the wheels and establish contact. For a pneumatic or electromechanical system, t_{\rm gear} = 0.5\text{–}1.0 s. The reference value t_{\rm gear} = 1.0 s is conservative.

I.4 Detection time. The SIAB detection time t_{\rm detect} = 0.1 s is a design requirement derived from the EBA standard.

I.5 Safe separation distance. For a vehicle density of N_{\rm veh} vehicles per km, the average spacing is d_{\rm sep} = 1000/N_{\rm veh} m. For N_{\rm veh} = 20, d_{\rm sep} = 50 m. The safety condition is:

d_{\rm stop}(v) < d_{\rm sep}.

I.6 Maximum speed. Substituting the stopping distance formula:

1.1 v + 0.125 v^2 < 50.

Solving the quadratic:

0.125 v^2 + 1.1 v - 50 = 0,

v = \frac{-1.1 + \sqrt{1.21 + 25}}{0.25} = \frac{-1.1 + 5.12}{0.25} = 16.08\ \text{m/s}.

The maximum safe speed is therefore v_{\max} \approx 16 m/s (57.9 km/h).

I.7 Sensitivity to parameters. If t_{\rm gear} = 0.5 s and a_{\rm brake} = 5 m/s²:

0.6 v + 0.1 v^2 < 50 \implies v_{\max} \approx 19.3\ \text{m/s}.

If t_{\rm gear} = 1.0 s and a_{\rm brake} = 3 m/s²:

1.1 v + 0.167 v^2 < 50 \implies v_{\max} \approx 13.6\ \text{m/s}.

The maximum speed is therefore in the range 13.6–19.3 m/s depending on the gear deployment time and braking deceleration. The reference value 16 m/s is central.

